  "slug": "learning-causation-2f3a",
  "summary": "Recent work adds uncertainty propagation, individual-level context, and ground-truth benchmarking to microbiome statistical analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is statistical methodology for microbiome data, where compositional structure and individual variation complicate inference. Taxon-in-context represents each microbe's context within an individual's gut microbiome, reporting individualized, age-dependent context. PARALLAX, an open-source R framework, propagates bacterial composition uncertainty through mixed models and stability metrics to improve differential abundance robustness and reproducibility. A benchmark of 18 co-occurrence network inference methods on synthetic 16S rDNA count data with known interaction networks evaluates their validity. Taxonova adds confidence-aware species-level amplicon classification, while canine k-mer analysis is a separate application. What is new is explicit treatment of uncertainty and individual context within analysis pipelines, alongside benchmarking against known ground truth. The items differ in analytical aim, so the shared element is methodological rigor rather than a single biological question.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-31"
}
